// APNT created-note seal, direct-exit branch v0.
//
// Maturity: stable — compiled by apnt_created_note_seal_exit_branch_v0.ts;
// not yet exercised by any published verify:* command. See AGENTS.md, "The
// maturity ladder".
//
// Target architecture:
// openspec/changes/define-apnt-private-spend-covenant-v0/design.md §1.2,
// with the coexistence framing in §3.1.
//
// This is the exit half of a created note's seal, and it is the deliberate
// opposite of the aggregate branch: a plain, ordinary, fully transparent BCH
// spend authorized by a one-time key. No proof verification, no covenant
// delegation, no transaction introspection, no token introspection. It exists
// so that a note holder is never dependent on any aggregator, prover, relay or
// counterparty to move their own funds — the non-custodial floor under the
// whole protocol (AGENTS.md "Hard constraints", design.md §3.1/§3.2).
//
// It is a PUBLIC, TRANSPARENT exit. Taking it forfeits the privacy the
// aggregate path provides. That is a stated protocol limitation, not a defect.
//
// Expected witness stack on entry (bottom to top):
//   [ signature65, exitPublicKey33 ]
//
// The committed key hash is substituted by the TypeScript builder into the
// marker below. It is `sha256(E33)` — PLAIN SHA-256, not
// `sha256DomainSeparated`, over the 33-byte compressed one-time exit public key
// `E`. That is deliberate (design.md §1.2): the domain-separated commitment the
// relation binds is a DIFFERENT value over the SAME key (design.md §4.3), so
// the on-chain preimage check stays two opcodes instead of five, and the two
// values are not interchangeable by construction.
//
// Two requirements this script makes that a naive "P2PK with a hash lock" would
// not, both load-bearing:
//
//   * exactly 65 bytes of signature (64-byte signature + 1 sighash byte).
//     This forces a Schnorr signature rather than a DER-encoded ECDSA one. It
//     makes the spend deterministically sized (real fee planning) and removes
//     signature malleability as a consideration.
//
//   * the trailing sighash byte is exactly 0x41 (SIGHASH_ALL | SIGHASH_FORKID).
//     Without this a spender could sign SIGHASH_NONE or SIGHASH_SINGLE and let
//     a miner or relay redirect the exit output. Both of those sighash types
//     are otherwise accepted by the VM's own signature-encoding check, so this
//     comparison is the only thing that rejects them.
//
// IMPLEMENTATION NOTE, measured and not assumed: BCH's OP_CHECKSIG verifies the
// EC-Schnorr-SHA256 construction specified for Bitcoin Cash (challenge
// `SHA256(r || compressedPubkey33 || m)`), NOT BIP-340 (which uses tagged
// hashes and a 32-byte x-only key). A BIP-340 signature over the same digest is
// rejected by the real BCH 2026 VM. See the reference-aggregator verification
// module for the executed measurement of both.
//
// This is a locking-template artifact. It is not APNT acceptance, note
// spendability, proof verification, wallet acceptance, or broadcast approval.
// Wiring this branch and the aggregate branch into one OP_IF/OP_ELSE/OP_ENDIF
// seal is a separate task and is deliberately NOT done here.

// --- require the supplied public key to be the committed one-time exit key ---
OP_DUP
OP_SHA256
@EXIT_KEY_HASH_PUSH@
OP_EQUALVERIFY

// --- require exactly 65 signature bytes: 64-byte Schnorr + 1 sighash byte ---
OP_SWAP
OP_SIZE <65> OP_NUMEQUALVERIFY

// --- require the sighash byte to be exactly SIGHASH_ALL | SIGHASH_FORKID ---
OP_DUP <64> OP_SPLIT OP_NIP
0x01 0x41 OP_EQUALVERIFY

// --- the one deliberate on-chain signature check in the protocol ---
OP_SWAP
OP_CHECKSIG
